# The markengine in the Editor

The **markengine** (`src/shared/lib/markengine/`) is the bridge between markdown
text and the block tree. The docs shell you are reading is rendered by it; the
editor uses it for something more demanding — turning what you type or paste into
live, editable blocks.

> The AST is the contract. Markdown becomes `BlockNode[]`, and every consumer —
> the typing shortcuts, the raw-mode importer, the read-only renderer — speaks
> that same shape.

## Two parsers, on purpose

The editor deliberately runs **two** parsers, each tuned for a different moment:

1. **`detectBlockType`** (`shortcutsDetect.ts`) — the **typing** parser. Fast,
   line-oriented, forgiving. It reads `> ` as a toggle and `"` / `|` as a quote,
   because that is what feels right mid-keystroke.
2. **`parseMarkdownToBlocks`** (`markdown/shortcuts.ts` → `parse` AST →
   `astToBlocks`) — the **paste / raw-mode Save** parser. A full CommonMark + GFM
   AST. Here `> ` is a *blockquote*, toggles are `> [>]`, and callouts are
   `> [!kind]`.

> [!warning] The same `>` means two things
> In the typing parser `> ` starts a toggle; in the AST parser `> ` is a quote.
> This is not a bug — it is the reason two parsers exist. Mixing them up is how
> round-trips lose block types. See [Serialization](serialization-round-trip.xmd).

## How the editor imports it

The markengine has **no package alias**. It is pulled in through the generic
`@ → src` alias:

```ts
import { parseMarkdownToBlocks } from "@/shared/lib/markengine";
```

Its core parser and renderers are pure Node functions with **zero external
dependencies**, which is exactly why they can run in a build script, in a test,
or in the browser without a DOM.

## A quirk worth knowing

The markengine sub-tree is **eslint-ignored** by the app, yet all of
`lib/markengine` still lives in the app's TypeScript compile graph — so a type
error there can break the quality gate even though the linter never looks at it.

> [!tip] See it end to end
> The exported functions are `parseMarkdownToBlocks`, `parseInlineMarkdown`, and
> `detectBlockType`. For the reverse direction — block tree back to markdown —
> read [Serialization & Round-Trip](serialization-round-trip.xmd).
